The network interface is configured in `/etc/network/interfaces`.

By default, DHCP is used to set up the Ethernet interface.

All units come with the same default MAC address: HWaddr D6:7D:AE:B3:0E:BA

The MAC address can be changed as follows:

auto eth0
iface eth0 inet dhcp
     hwaddress ether 02:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx

Choose a locally administered MAC: set the second least significant bit of the first octet (e.g. 02:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx is
fine) to avoid collisions with real vendor MACs. For example:

02:1A:3C:7E:90:11
02:1A:3C:7E:90:12
02:1A:3C:7E:90:13
02:1A:3C:7E:90:14
02:1A:3C:7E:90:15

A static address can be set up as follows:

iface eth0 inet static
     address 192.168.0.42
     network 192.168.0.0
     netmask 255.255.255.0
     broadcast 192.168.0.255
     gateway 192.168.0.1

See also https://unix.stackexchange.com/questions/128439/good-detailed-explanation-of-etc-network-interfaces-syntax
